   L1248: conclusIon
   L1249: The variegated understandings of energy services, vulnerability, justice and
   L1250: transitions reviewed above all point to the multiple ways in which house-
>> L1251: hold susceptibilities to energy poverty are determined by the material
   L1252: characteristics of residential locations and neighbourhoods. These are
   L1253: highly spatially uneven at a variety of scales (Bouzarovski & Cauvain,
   L1254: 2016) while being embedded in political systems and decision-making
   L1255: structures. The multiple spatially embedded characteristics of the place in
   L1256: which people live—including less known issues such as inflexible heating
   L1257: systems, energy-inefficient buildings and a lack of access to more suitable
   L1258: [PAGE 41]
>> L1259: UNDERSTANDING ENERGY POVERTY, VULNERABILITY AND JUSTICE 29
   L1260: energy carriers—assemble to create situations of inadequate energy ser-
   L1261: vices and high costs (Maxim, Mihai, Apostoaie, & Maxim, 2017).
   L1262: Like other forms of inequality (Dorling & Ballas, 2008; Walker, 2009),
>> L1263: therefore, energy poverty is a deeply geographical and political phenom-
   L1264: enon. It is unequally distributed and experienced across different places,
   L1265: and is articulated through complex politics of distribution and recogni-
   L1266: tion. One of the main implications of these arguments is that, in terms of
>> L1267: vulnerability to energy poverty, where a person lives seems at least as sig-
   L1268: nificant as the socio-economic group that they are part of—yet in much of
   L1269: the current literature and policy discourse inequalities and vulnerability
   L1270: tend to be defined in terms of the latter, rather than in socio-technical,
   L1271: housing or locality terms (Moore, 2012). Thus, spatially uneven patterns
>> L1272: of energy poverty are the result of processes and injustices operating
   L1273: throughout the whole energy system, along with economic, material and
   L1274: cultural inequalities acting at various scales. This disrupts the produc-
   L1275: tion vs. consumption binary (Jenkins et al., 2016) that has traditionally
   L1276: dominated energy studies, while calling attention to the need for under-
   L1277: standing how power interests, relations and processes contribute to the
   L1278: rise of energy-related inequalities.
   L1279: I have also argued that energy transitions are generators of geographi-
   L1280: cally uneven social, political and environmental displacements. These may
   L1281: increase the vulnerability of particular social groups or places; a contin-
   L1282: gency that is of special relevance to the global movement towards a low
>> L1283: carbon future. The geographies of energy poverty, vulnerability and jus-
   L1284: tice, therefore, embody a distinct temporal dimension. The corollary of
   L1285: this claim is that identifying vulnerable areas also needs to take into
   L1286: account predicted changes in energy prices, forms of infrastructure provi-
   L1287: sion and economic inequality. The two chapters that follow return to
   L1288: the European context via a critical examination of the policy context that
>> L1289: allows energy poverty to be being addressed and regulated, while review-
   L1290: ing existing knowledge about the extent and nature of the condition
   L1291: across Europe.
