   L3792: conclusIon
>> L3793: There is little doubt that energy poverty is a pervasive problem across the
   L3794: EU, and is likely to expand in coming years as a result of anticipated energy
   L3795: price increases. For a long time, however, systematic research on issues of
   L3796: domestic energy deprivation in the much of the EU was scarce, especially
   L3797: in the countries of ECE and the Mediterranean where this condition is
   L3798: likely to be most pronounced. This means that, other than the UK and the
   L3799: RoI—which have a longer tradition of academic scholarship and policy
>> L3800: frameworks to address the issue—energy poverty measures in many EU
   L3801: Member States are of an emergent nature.
   L3802: Understanding the causes, content and consequences of European
>> L3803: energy poverty is all the more pertinent in light of the increasing policy
   L3804: attention that is being paid to the issue within various EU institutions. In
   L3805: contrast to many mainstream efforts to reduce the problem to affordability
