   L4136: geographical characteristics of cities, regions and countries themselves
   L4137: combine to produce and sustain this particular form of injustice.
   L4138: As we argued in Bouzarovski et al. (2017) the emergence of energy
   L4139: vulnerability of a distinct spatial formation involves the interplay between
   L4140: concurrent processes of social change on the one hand, and the tangible
   L4141: and intangible features of particular places, on the other. At the same time,
>> L4142: the dynamics that allow energy poverty to arise and persist within specific
   L4143: material sites also shape wider political and social processes—as well as
   L4144: processes of institutional change in the energy sector itself—via an addi-
>> L4145: tional feedback loop. Thus, the driving forces of energy poverty and vul-
   L4146: nerability in socio-spatial terms are multidirectional and multiscalar. They
   L4147: involve different temporal and spatial horizons, while demonstrating the
   L4148: ability to shape broader political dynamics.
